items(240,[
I('P000375-P000376','definition','Algebraic order of a differential operator',r'Vinogradov proposes an algebraic theory over any ring, explicitly allowing a noncommutative ring. For an additive map \(A:M\to N\), the notes define \((kA)m=kA(m)\), \((k^+A)m=A(km)\), and \(\delta_k=k^+-k\); order at most \(n\) means every \((n+1)\)-fold iterated \(\delta\) vanishes.',r'Let \(K\) be a commutative unital algebra over a commutative base \(R\), and let \(M,N\) be unital \(K\)-modules. An \(R\)-linear map \(A:M\to N\) has order at most \(n\) when \(\delta_{k_0}\cdots\delta_{k_n}A=0\) for all \(k_j\in K\). Order zero means \(K\)-linearity. Additive operators correspond to the base \(R=\mathbb Z\). For a noncommutative ring these same formulas do not furnish the ensuing commutative two-module calculus without additional choices.',r'The commutator definition predates this lecture: \citet[\S16.8]{grothendieck1967egaiv4} was published in 1967. The closely matching presentation in \citet[\S1]{vinogradov1972logic}, received 19 January 1972, explicitly assumes a commutative ring with identity; that hypothesis is essential to its subsequent functors.',r'the commutative definition is standard. Noncommutative differential calculi require a specified replacement; the notebook’s unrestricted wording is not a theorem extending the entire construction.',literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='exact',refs=G+V[:1]),
I('P000376','theorem','Agreement with differential operators on vector bundles',r'The notes state that the algebraic definition agrees with the classical one.',r'For a smooth finite-dimensional manifold \(X\), take \(K=C^\infty(X,\mathbb R)\), \(R=\mathbb R\), and \(M=\Gamma(E),N=\Gamma(F)\) for smooth finite-rank vector bundles. The \(\mathbb R\)-linear operators satisfying the iterated-commutator criterion of order at most \(n\) are exactly the usual linear differential operators of that order. This assertion concerns section modules, not arbitrary algebraic modules over \(C^\infty(X)\).',r'\citet[\S1]{vinogradov1972logic} states this comparison as part of the announced programme; \citet[Theorem 2.2]{vinogradov2015logic} restates it. The classical differential operators themselves were already familiar; Class A concerns this lecture’s published algebraic presentation.',r'the comparison is established under the stated smooth hypotheses. Identification of every universal algebraic form with an ordinary smooth form requires a further category restriction, discussed in occurrence 1971-241.3.',**{'class':'A'},publication_match='exact',refs=V),
I('P000376','construction','Cojet corepresentation and its evaluation operator',r'The right module \(\operatorname{Diff}_n^+(M,N)\) is written as \(\operatorname{Hom}_K(M,\operatorname{Diff}_n^+N)\), with \(\operatorname{Diff}_n^+N=\operatorname{Diff}_n^+(K,N)\). The latter object is called cojets.',r'For commutative unital \(K/R\), put \(C_n(N)=\operatorname{Diff}_{K/R,n}(K,N)\) with scalar action \((a\cdot B)(b)=B(ab)\). There is a natural \(K\)-module isomorphism \(\operatorname{Diff}_n^+(M,N)\cong\operatorname{Hom}_K(M,C_n(N))\), given by \(A\mapsto[m\mapsto(b\mapsto A(bm))]\). Its inverse is composition with \(\epsilon(B)=B(1)\), an operator of order at most \(n\).',r'This exact cojet formulation is printed in \citet[\S1]{vinogradov1972logic}. The November 8 continuation supplies its proof in six elementary lemmas, recorded separately as occurrences 1971-242.1--6.',r'the natural isomorphism is established. The precomposition action is indispensable: replacing it by pointwise multiplication would make the displayed currying map generally nonlinear over \(K\).',**{'class':'A'},publication_match='exact',refs=V[:1]),
I('P000376','construction','Jets represent differential operators in the target variable',r'The notes pair the cojet formula with \(\operatorname{Diff}_n(M,N)=\operatorname{Hom}(J^n(M),N)\) and announce existence of jets.',r'For commutative \(K/R\), a universal map \(j_n:M\to J^n_{K/R}(M)\) of order at most \(n\) induces \(\operatorname{Hom}_K(J^n_{K/R}(M),N)\cong\operatorname{Diff}_{K/R,n}(M,N)\) naturally in \(N\). Here the operator module uses multiplication on its values. Existence follows from the quotient construction written explicitly in the next lecture; no finite generation or projectivity is required for algebraic module representability.',r'Principal parts and their universal property are older, in \citet[\S16.8]{grothendieck1967egaiv4}. \citet[\S1]{vinogradov1972logic} places the two universal properties side by side. The construction is expository as a representability fact.',r'the algebraic universal property is established. Being a finite-rank geometric jet bundle is a stronger smooth or regularity statement.',publication_match='exact',refs=G+V[:1]),
I('P000376','claim','The algebra generated by the two scalar actions',r'The notes suggest constructing \(J^n(K)\) as the algebra generated by \(k\) and \(k^+\) on \(\operatorname{Diff}_nK\); a lower index in the formula remains uncertain.',r'The canonical algebraic construction is \(J^n_{K/R}(K)=(K\otimes_R K)/I^{n+1}\), where \(I=\ker(K\otimes_RK\to K)\). The two factors give the two scalar actions and their differences generate \(I\). An action of this quotient on an operator module need not be faithful, so identifying the universal algebra with its image on one chosen module needs a separate argument.',r'This is the principal-parts construction of \citet[\S16.8]{grothendieck1967egaiv4}; the jet quotient is also given in \citet[Proposition 7.1]{vinogradov2015logic}. Neither source justifies an unrestricted faithfulness claim for the notebook’s chosen representation.',r'the universal quotient is established; the exact intended lower index and whether equality with an operator image was meant remain uncertain.',reconstruction=r'The diagonal-ideal quotient is a mathematical reconstruction, not a claimed reading of the crossed notation.',publication_match='partial',refs=G+V[1:],confidence='medium'),
I('P000377','construction','Recursive multiderivation functors',r'Before the 1 November heading, the notes define \(D_1(N)=\operatorname{Der}(K,N)\), explain that it is a left submodule but only a subset of \(\operatorname{Diff}_1^+N\), and put \(D_i(N)=D\{D_{i-1}(N)\subset(\operatorname{Diff}_1^+)^{i-1}N\}\).',r'For a subset \(S\) of a \(K\)-module \(Q\), write \(D(S\subset Q)\) for the \(R\)-derivations \(K\to Q\) whose values lie in \(S\). Starting with \(D_0(N)=N\), the notebook’s recursive definition uses the indicated precomposition module at each stage. It cannot be replaced by repeated application of the ordinary left-module functor \(D\). In the smooth geometric category it produces multivector fields with coefficients in \(N\).',r'The recursive construction and the warning that the intermediate subset is not a right submodule appear in \citet[\S2]{vinogradov1972logic}; the later explanation is \citet[\S5.2]{vinogradov2015logic}.',r'this construction is established in the commutative setting. Its representing objects are forms; the functor values are multiderivations, a distinction blurred in the next lecture.',**{'class':'A'},publication_match='exact',refs=V)
])
